/* Pass 13 A: early Maui slow-burn, memory and community scenes. */
(function(){"use strict";const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const revisions={"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-03":{"number":3,"content":"The bell under Lanternwell Square was not supposed to ring.\n\nThat was what Neri, the valley's oldest resident, told us as we knelt beside the spring. Neri had grey curls, a walking stick cut from dark wood, and the unsettling habit of noticing questions before people asked them.\n\n“It hasn't sounded since the last lost traveler found their way,” they said.\n\n“Lost traveler?” I repeated.\n\nNeri looked at Maui. Maui suddenly became interested in examining a crack in the stone.\n\n“The valley does not take people,” Neri explained. “Doors open. Travelers choose. Sometimes someone arrives before they remember the road behind them.”\n\n“Can the spring tell me who I was?”\n\n“It can show what is attached to the key. Whether you wish to look is yours.”\n\nI unwound the blue thread at my wrist. The brass key slid into my palm. There was a tiny notch near its teeth, shaped like a half-moon.\n\nNeri unfolded a small cloth beside the spring and set it across the stone.\n\n\"People often imagine a spring like this gives answers,\" they said. \"It doesn't speak in sentences. Sometimes it reflects something attached to the object you bring. Sometimes it shows nothing.\"\n\n\"What happens if I don't like what I see?\"\n\n\"Then we stop.\"\n\nMaui looked toward the water.\n\n\"Simple.\"\n\n\"Not always,\" Neri said. \"People can become desperate for an answer. I have seen them keep asking long after they were ready to listen.\"\n\nI touched the half-moon notch in the key.\n\n\"Would it hurt the spring to try?\"\n\n\"No. But it might hurt you to see something before you're ready. That is why I'm explaining it first.\"\n\nI thought about the way my mind had gone blank when I reached for a name. I had spent every night since the beach feeling that absence like a missing tooth. Part of me wanted to thrust the key into the water and demand that the whole world give me back whatever had been taken.\n\nAnother part wanted to keep walking and never ask again.\n\nNeri said, \"You could come back tomorrow.\"\n\nMaui shifted his lantern to his other hand.\n\n\"Or next week. The spring isn't going anywhere.\"\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"Pretty sure. It would be difficult to move this much stone.\"\n\nI laughed despite the tightness in my chest.\n\nI looked at the spring again.\n\n\"I want to try now,\" I said.\n\nNeri nodded, then stepped back.\n\nMaui held the lantern while I approached the water. He did not reach for my hand. I was beginning to understand that his restraint was deliberate.\n\nThe moment the key touched the spring, a bell rang beneath the stones.\n\nI saw a kitchen with a window full of rain. A table covered in papers. Someone telling me I would always be the dependable one. The memory flashed away before I could decide whether the voice had been loving or demanding.\n\nThen came a word, clear as a pebble hitting the bottom of a well.\n\nJasper.\n\nI stumbled. Maui caught my elbow only after I reached for him.\n\n“That's my name,” I whispered.\n\nNeri waited.\n\nI said it again, testing the sound against the shape of myself. “Jasper.”\n\nMaui tried it with exaggerated solemnity. “Jasper. Excellent name. Strong. Dramatic. Goes with heroic companions.”\n\n“I'm standing right here.”\n\n“Which proves my point.”\n\nI laughed, and the sound broke something open that fear had kept shut.\n\nFor a long time I stood there listening to the ordinary water.\n\nThe bell had fallen silent. People were going about their business beyond the fountain: someone lifting a basket of bread, a pair of neighbors discussing a leaking roof, a child laughing as a paper lantern turned in the breeze. Nobody stopped what they were doing to insist I explain what the spring had shown.\n\nI wondered how many of them had arrived by a door they didn't understand.\n\nNeri watched my face.\n\n\"Will you write the name in the arrival book?\" they asked.\n\nI hesitated.\n\n\"What happens if I do?\"\n\n\"It helps us address you correctly and return things you borrow. It doesn't mean you belong to the valley, and it doesn't give anyone authority over where you travel.\"\n\n\"Could I change it?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nI looked at the book on the nearby table. There were empty lines between names, not the kind of narrow crowded spaces that made a person feel as though they had to squeeze themselves to fit.\n\n\"Then I want to write it myself.\"\n\nNeri handed me the pen.\n\nMy first attempt left a blot of ink beside the J. My hand shook, and I hated it for shaking. I almost reached for a fresh page.\n\n\"Can I leave the blot?\" I asked.\n\n\"It's your line.\"\n\nI wrote the rest.\n\nJasper.\n\nThe letters weren't elegant. They were mine.\n\nMaui leaned over the table.\n\n\"That looks very official.\"\n\n\"It's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Official handwriting.\"\n\nI nudged him with my elbow.\n\nNeri's registry had a second column I hadn't noticed. Beside the names were little marks showing whether a traveler wanted someone notified that they'd arrived.\n\n\"What does this symbol mean?\" I asked.\n\n\"It means the person chose not to have a message sent.\"\n\nMaui glanced at the page, then deliberately looked away when Neri moved it closer to me. He was trying; I could tell by the very serious expression he wore.\n\n\"Can I leave mine blank until I know who I might tell?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" Neri said. \"A name and an address are not the same thing.\"\n\nIt was a small distinction, but it loosened something in my chest. I had written Jasper without having to provide a history to justify it. I could decide later whether to contact the people behind the door, or whether to ask someone I trusted to help me do it.\n\nMaui pointed to the wet ink. \"What if you change your handwriting?\"\n\n\"Then my name won't fall off.\"\n\n\"Excellent. I had been concerned.\"\n\nI laughed. Neri slid the page beneath blotting paper and gave me a separate card for questions I wanted to ask before a future crossing. I wrote one: *Who can see my information?* The fact that I could ask that without being ungrateful for their help felt unexpectedly important.\n\nWhen we left, the bell remained quiet. My name didn't need to make it ring for the day to matter.\n\nHe grinned, then grew unexpectedly quiet.\n\n\"Would you rather I didn't tell anyone what happened?\" he asked.\n\nI looked up.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you might want to decide when people hear the story.\"\n\nI thought about it. The name was good news; the glimpse of the rainy kitchen was something I wasn't ready to unpack in front of strangers.\n\n\"You can tell them I remembered my name. Not the picture I saw.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Done.\"\n\nIt was a small agreement, made beside a fountain with somebody waiting to carry bread through the square. I had no idea yet how much I'd come to value it.\n\nI closed the arrival book gently and handed the pen back.\n\nThe spring held no more pictures. I expected disappointment, but I felt a strange tenderness toward the one small thing it had given me.\n\n“Does knowing your name help?” Maui asked as we crossed the square.\n\n“Yes. No. Maybe.” I looked at the houses. “I don't know if I liked who I was.”\n\nHe stopped near a row of lanterns.\n\n“Then don't decide that today.”\n\n“What if I never remember?”\n\n“Then you'll still be Jasper, if you want to be.”\n\nI stared at him. People who called themselves heroes were supposed to tell you what came next. Maui, who clearly adored the sound of his own voice, had somehow left the important space empty.\n\n“Is that all?” I asked.\n\n“Nope. Now I'm going to brag about how I helped the most fascinating person on this coast discover a name. It'll take at least twenty minutes.”\n\n“You didn't discover it. I did.”\n\nHe smiled, sharp and delighted.\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nThat evening I took the brass key to the little room Neri had offered above the Lanternwell bakery. I wrote my name on a scrap of paper and pinned it beside the bed, not because I feared forgetting it again but because I liked being able to choose where it belonged.\n\nDown below, Maui was telling someone a very inaccurate version of our morning.\n\nI opened the window.\n\n“You left out the part where you nearly fell in the spring!”\n\nHe looked up, grinning.\n\n“Artistic license!”\n\nFor the first time, the name Jasper felt less like a missing label and more like an introduction.","title":"The Name Beneath the Bell","word_count":1433},"maui-04":{"number":4,"content":"There were six lanterns along the eastern footbridge, and three were broken.\n\nNeri offered to find a carpenter. Maui offered to carry the entire bridge to a different river. I asked to see the fittings.\n\nThe first bolt came loose beneath my fingers. I took apart the little brass housing, checked the wick assembly, and sketched a safer support on the back of a flour receipt. I didn't know how I knew where the strain would travel. My hand simply drew the answer.\n\nMaui leaned close enough to see without crowding me.\n\n“Were you an inventor?”\n\n“Maybe.”\n\n“A locksmith? A shipwright? A magnificent criminal mastermind?”\n\n“Please don't get excited about that last possibility.”\n\n“Too late.”\n\nIt took considerably longer than my sketch suggested.\n\nThe housing was simple enough on paper: a hinge, a wick cup, a bracket, and a bolt that should have caught on the inner lip. In practice, the first lantern had been patched twice with whatever metal the village could spare. One screw had a square head; its neighbor was almost round. A thin crack ran through the support behind the glass.\n\nNeri brought a stool and a wooden box of salvaged fittings. They knelt beside me without making the repair sound like a trial.\n\n\"We don't have much brass,\" they said. \"The smith can make more, but not before the market delivery.\"\n\n\"Is this all the stock?\"\n\n\"For now.\"\n\nI turned a mismatched bolt in my fingers. \"Then I want to test a wooden spacer instead of using two of the good ones. Only if somebody can tell me how this wood holds up in rain.\"\n\nPip, who had been carrying tools between the bakery and bridge, raised a hand.\n\n\"My granddad treats shutters with oil. I can ask what kind.\"\n\nI nearly said no out of habit. It would be quicker to invent an answer than wait for one.\n\n\"Would you?\" I asked.\n\nPip ran off, delighted to be useful.\n\nMaui leaned over the railing. \"I could fetch an entire tree.\"\n\n\"We need a spacer the size of my thumb.\"\n\n\"Very small tree.\"\n\n\"How about you hold the rail while I check the bracket?\"\n\n\"At last. Work suited to my magnitude.\"\n\nHe crouched to brace the loose section. Even bent nearly double, he managed to look as though someone had invited him to a public ceremony.\n\nThe first lantern flickered when I tested it. The flame leaned toward the glass and left a trail of soot. I felt the awful urge to announce a solution anyway, because people were watching and I wanted them to stop.\n\n\"What's wrong?\" Neri asked.\n\n\"Air,\" I said. \"It doesn't have enough air coming through the lower vent. If I close the top now, it'll smoke and heat the housing.\"\n\n\"So the repair isn't finished.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey nodded. \"Then we'll leave it unlit tonight. A warning sign is cheaper than replacing a burned bridge.\"\n\nSomething in me loosened. I had been expecting disappointment; what I received was a sensible answer.\n\nPip returned with a note from the shutter-maker and two small offcuts of treated timber. We made a spacer. I filed its edge twice and got the angle wrong once. Maui pretended not to notice until I caught him squinting at my hands.\n\n\"You may say it,\" I told him.\n\n\"The small tree is winning.\"\n\n\"Apparently.\"\n\nI tried again. This time the bracket sat straight and the vent remained clear. Neri asked for a drawing of the repair so the next person wouldn't need me standing beside them. I made one, labeling every part I could name and drawing a question mark beside the fitting I couldn't.\n\nI wanted to erase the question mark.\n\nI left it there.\n\nThe bridge belonged to people who would cross it long after I'd gone. They deserved to know what I did not know.\n\nBy afternoon I'd repaired two lanterns and convinced Maui to stop holding pieces directly over my head. He could lift anything; that did not make him the ideal person to hold every tool.\n\nWhen the third lantern lit, several neighbors clapped. I smiled, then found myself apologizing because one housing was slightly crooked.\n\nMaui blinked.\n\n“Why are you sorry?”\n\n“I should have done it better.”\n\n“It's working.”\n\n“I know, but—”\n\n“But what?”\n\nI tried to answer. The remembered voice returned, without a face: If you have the ability, you have the responsibility. Don't make this about you.\n\nMy stomach tightened.\n\nMaui's expression changed. He set down the last tool.\n\nNeri came back with the village smith, a woman named Ressa who inspected the new fittings without praising anybody.\n\n\"The hinges are better,\" she said. \"This one's still catching.\"\n\nI felt heat climb my face. \"I can redo it.\"\n\n\"Yes, you can. But first tell me why it catches.\"\n\nShe held the lantern steady while I moved the door. The brass dragged against the repaired bracket at the bottom, where I'd made the angle too narrow. I'd been so relieved that the flame was protected that I hadn't checked what happened when someone actually needed to change the wick.\n\nMaui peered over my shoulder. \"So it works, except when people use it?\"\n\n\"That's an uncharitable summary.\"\n\n\"An accurate one,\" Ressa said.\n\nI laughed despite myself. We loosened the bolt, made the adjustment, then asked a market worker to open the housing while wearing gloves. They found the catch on the first attempt and shut it again without scraping their knuckles.\n\n\"Now it works,\" Ressa said. She charged the council for her inspection. Neri wrote the expense down instead of turning it into a thank-you that could not buy the smith's supper.\n\nMaui watched me sign the work sheet.\n\n\"Do your hands remember how to be pleased?\" he asked.\n\n\"Apparently not.\"\n\n\"Could we teach them?\"\n\n\"Not with a monument.\"\n\nHe pretended to be devastated. I looked up at the lantern we'd fixed, its new glow steadier than the old one. The work had needed another person's knowledge. It had still been my work.\n\n\"Maybe with a cup of tea,\" I said.\n\nHe brightened. \"I can acquire tea.\"\n\n\"Buy it, Maui. From the person selling it.\"\n\nHe looked briefly offended, then grinned. We went to the stall together, and I was able to enjoy the light behind us without looking for the next mistake.\n\n“Hey. You get to be pleased when you make something good.”\n\n“It isn't that simple.”\n\n“Then tell me the complicated version.”\n\nI couldn't. Not yet.\n\nInstead I handed him a cup of water, remembering he'd forgotten to drink while helping. He accepted it, then held it up in a mock toast.\n\n“To the person who repaired the bridge.”\n\n“You did the heavy lifting.”\n\n“Yes, and I was incredible. But I'm not stealing your applause.”\n\nThat evening we crossed the bridge together. The lanterns illuminated a garden Neri had planted below. I stopped to admire a row of herbs, and Maui followed my gaze.\n\n“You like growing things?”\n\n“I think so.”\n\n“Good. We'll find you a patch of ground. One you can make mistakes in without apologizing to the dirt.”\n\nI laughed hard enough to lean against the railing.\n\nMaui looked pleased, but a little shy when I caught him watching me. It was the first time I'd seen pride turn uncertain on his face.\n\n“You really don't mind that I don't remember?” I asked.\n\n“I mind that it hurts you.”\n\n“That wasn't my question.”\n\n“No.” He looked at the river. “I don't mind not knowing everything about you.”\n\n“That's a first for a demigod, I imagine.”\n\n“I contain multitudes.”\n\nI watched the water carry the broken reflections of three new lanterns downstream.\n\n“Thank you,” I said.\n\nHis smile softened.\n\n“Anytime, Jasper.”\n\nIt was the first time he'd said my name without making it part of a performance. That somehow mattered more than all his grand speeches put together.","title":"The Things Hands Remember","word_count":1309},"maui-05":{"number":5,"content":"Maui's fishhook could move stone, calm a snarled rope, and turn its owner into an alarming number of animals. It could not persuade a tiny brass compass to point north.\n\n“Defective,” he announced.\n\n“You keep holding it beside the hook.”\n\n“Which should make it grateful.”\n\nThe compass was Neri's. It had begun spinning three days earlier, when a Threadway opened at the foot of Tideglass Cove and let in a cold tide from somewhere none of us recognized. The current was taking sand from the beach and pulling it toward a crack beneath the rocks.\n\nIf it continued, the little harbor would lose its shelter.\n\nMaui wanted to plug the crack with a boulder. I wanted to map the current first.\n\nWe argued on the shore while seabirds wheeled overhead.\n\n“You're making a plan for a puddle,” he said.\n\n“It's an inlet with moving water.”\n\n“Very threatening water.”\n\n“It might be, if you stop pretending the only answer is being bigger than the problem.”\n\nHis face closed.\n\nI regretted the sharpness before I had finished speaking, but I did not apologize for the idea.\n\n“I'm going to measure it,” I said. “You can help, or not.”\n\nI took string, weights, and strips of colored cloth down the beach. After a long silence, Maui picked up the heaviest bundle.\n\n“Tell me where,” he said.\n\nFor two hours we tracked the current. The water was not trying to drag the shore away. It was being pulled through a doorway beneath the rocks, one that had opened unevenly and become stuck.\n\nMaui knelt beside the opening.\n\n“So. Boulder's a bad idea.”\n\n“Spectacularly.”\n\nHe gave me a narrow look. “Enjoy this moment. It is rare.”\n\nWe could not decide what to call the doorway.\n\nNeri said *tide arch*, because the old survey marked the waterline beside it. Maui said *the world's worst bathtub plug*, which was not a name I intended to write in the records. I called it *the lower Tideglass Threadway* on my first page and then underlined *provisional* beside the name.\n\nFen, the tidekeeper, met us on the sand with a weighted cord and a sour expression.\n\n\"You're both standing where the bank slipped yesterday,\" they said.\n\nMaui looked at the sand beneath his feet. \"This bank?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe moved. I followed him, somewhat less proudly.\n\nFen showed us yesterday's marks: a dark line left by high tide, a fan of pebbles where the current had turned, and a narrow groove cut through the sediment. Whatever the unstable opening was doing, it wasn't merely making a compass misbehave. It was redirecting the water that sheltered fishing boats.\n\n\"If we block it,\" Fen said, \"the pressure will look for another weakness. If we leave it, the sand will move out of the harbor.\"\n\nMaui frowned. \"So both choices are bad.\"\n\n\"Unmeasured choices are bad,\" Fen replied. \"Let's find one we can understand.\"\n\nI liked Fen immediately.\n\nWe tied strips of blue and red cloth to separate lengths of line and set them in the current from a safe point on the rock. The red strip swung toward the inlet and stayed there; the blue one tugged outward, catching a counterflow against the submerged stone. I recorded their movements while Fen called tide intervals. Maui held the weighted rope steady with a care I hadn't expected.\n\nMy pencil broke.\n\n\"Of course,\" I muttered.\n\nMaui handed me a charcoal stub from his pouch.\n\n\"You carry charcoal?\" I asked.\n\n\"For writing my own magnificent praises.\"\n\n\"Naturally.\"\n\n\"Also for fire-starting.\"\n\n\"Less magnificent.\"\n\n\"Not when you're cold.\"\n\nI drew a second diagram on the back of Neri's discarded receipt. The pattern made sense: the bent ring was keeping one side of the arch open. Water entered at the wrong moment, not as an ordinary tide but as a narrow, powerful crosscurrent. The harbor did not need a heroic boulder. It needed that hinge seated correctly, and it needed a way to check that the fix held after the tide changed.\n\nFen studied the sketch.\n\n\"Can you reach the ring without standing in the pull?\"\n\n\"Only if Maui lifts the outer slab,\" I said. \"And only if you keep the line attached to both of us.\"\n\nMaui opened his mouth to object to needing a rope.\n\nFen raised an eyebrow.\n\nHe closed it.\n\n\"Fine,\" he said. \"A very heroic rope.\"\n\nThe first attempt failed. The slab shifted, grit slid across the ring, and I had to pull my hand back before the metal caught my sleeve. Maui held the weight while Fen called a halt.\n\n\"Again?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"Not until we check the tether,\" Fen replied.\n\nI was shaking. It took me an embarrassing moment to admit it.\n\n\"Could we stop for five minutes?\" I asked.\n\nMaui looked at me. \"Of course.\"\n\nDuring the break, Fen unrolled a second chart across the rock. It showed where the current ran at low tide and how far it shifted when the cold Threadway water pushed through.\n\n\"There's a difference between a temporary surge and a changed channel,\" they said. \"If we force that ring now, we might get through the first one and still leave the harbor worse next week.\"\n\nI traced the lines with a finger. The hand that had drawn the little lantern bracket the day before wanted to leap ahead to a solution. My legs were trembling from the first attempt.\n\n\"Can we compare it with yesterday's waterline?\" I asked.\n\nFen pointed to a chalk mark under the rock ledge. \"That's why we measured it.\"\n\nMaui crouched beside us. He looked at the chalk, then at the boulder he'd suggested using as a plug.\n\n\"So if I block the opening, the water finds somewhere else.\"\n\n\"Possibly through somebody's landing,\" Fen said.\n\nHe sighed. \"My boulder has become unpopular.\"\n\nI wanted to tease him. Instead I passed him the rope and asked if he'd help mark the next safe position.\n\nHe tied one knot, then stopped. \"This kind?\"\n\n\"Show Fen.\"\n\nFen checked it and made him redo the loose turn. He didn't argue. When the rope finally held the strain under a controlled pull, he looked delighted in a quieter way than I'd ever seen.\n\n\"Is this what ordinary sailors do?\" he asked.\n\n\"Among other things,\" Fen said.\n\n\"Exhausting.\"\n\nI laughed, and the tension in my shoulders eased. We still had a dangerous mechanism to inspect. Nobody had made it safe just by being brave. But we had a measured window, a better tether, and permission to stop when the conditions changed.\n\nWhen we tried again, I counted aloud rather than letting everyone imagine they knew what I was thinking. Maui waited for the count. That, more than his strength, made me trust the attempt.\n\nI sat on the high dry rock and drank water. My hands had remembered the shape of the mechanism, but they still belonged to a person who could be frightened. Nobody called me weak. Fen marked the next safe window, and Maui sat beside me without turning my pause into a lecture.\n\n\"Still think the boulder would work?\" I asked.\n\n\"Absolutely,\" he said. \"For making a larger disaster.\"\n\nI laughed, then felt brave enough to stand again.\n\nOn the second attempt, we reset the ring a fraction at a time. I asked Maui to shift the stone; he waited for my instruction. Fen watched the tide marks and gave us room to stop whenever the water changed. When the blue cloth released from the pull, all three of us saw it.\n\n\"Hold,\" Fen called.\n\nWe held.\n\nThe ring settled. The line slackened.\n\nWe didn't celebrate until Fen finished the last measurement.\n\nWe worked together. He shifted the rocks enough for me to reach the bent brass ring at the heart of the door. I adjusted it, checked where the pressure would go, and used the blue thread from my old key to mark the safe alignment. When Maui returned the last stone, the inlet took a breath and settled.\n\nThe compass stopped spinning.\n\nAt sunset, we sat above the cove with sand in our shoes. I nudged his shoulder.\n\n“You didn't have to let me win the argument.”\n\n“I didn't.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“You were right,” he said. “That's different.”\n\nThe admission cost him something. I could see it in the way his hand tightened around the hook.\n\n“Does being wrong hurt?” I asked.\n\nHe laughed without humor. “When everyone expects you to save them? Yes.”\n\nI thought of the voice from my memory, the one that had taught me usefulness was a debt.\n\n“I know that feeling,” I said.\n\nFor a while we watched the water go where it was meant to go.\n\nThen Maui asked whether I wanted him to teach me to steer by stars that changed their positions every night. I asked whether he could actually do that.\n\n“No,” he said. “But it sounds like a magnificent challenge.”\n\nI took his offered hand to stand.\n\nFor once the horizon didn't seem to be demanding that either of us prove we deserved to be standing in front of it.","title":"Where the Water Goes","word_count":1510},"maui-06":{"number":6,"content":"The first visitors from Aotearoa reached the Lanternweald on an evening of mist.\n\nThey came through a Threadway carrying instruments, wrapped food, and stories they had chosen to share. Their organizer, Mereana, introduced herself as a Māori performer and teacher. She spoke carefully about her community, her own teachers, and the fact that no single person could speak for every iwi or every tradition.\n\nMaui tried to greet her with the sort of theatrical flourish he usually reserved for a crowd.\n\nMereana smiled. “I have met impressive storytellers before.”\n\n“Impossible. Nobody this impressive.”\n\n“Then perhaps you'll enjoy listening for a change.”\n\nI had to turn away to hide my grin.\n\nThe gathering took place beneath a roof of woven branches beside Lanternwell Square. Mereana spoke of the whare tapere, places associated with storytelling, recreation, music, movement, and meetings between people, including lovers in some traditions. Her group offered a contemporary presentation: a poi performance shaped around a story of separation and reunion, paired with a waiata aroha they had chosen for the evening.\n\nShe explained that waiata aroha could carry longing, grief, or love; poi had its own histories and purposes; and haka was not a generic courtship routine. This evening's piece was their artistic interpretation, not a claim to perform an ancient universal wedding ritual.\n\nI listened to the rhythm of the poi and the steadiness of the performers. The motions were practiced, the story held in a community, not borrowed for someone else's decoration.\n\nBeside me Maui went quiet.\n\nAfterward, Mereana invited questions. Maui asked about the history of the performance with a seriousness that surprised me. He did not pretend that all Polynesian islands shared one identical custom. He listened when she spoke of Aotearoa and of the traditions particular to her people.\n\nWhen the visitors began serving food, Maui drew me aside toward the edge of the square.\n\n“I had a big speech planned,” he admitted.\n\n“That's shocking.”\n\n“I know. But then I realized it was their night to tell a story.”\n\n“That's almost growth.”\n\nHe put a hand over his heart. “You wound me.”\n\nWe walked between the lanterns while distant voices carried from the gathering. Maui looked at his tattoos.\n\n“Every mark says what I did,” he said. “People think that means they know everything about me.”\n\nI thought of the name I'd found in the spring, and all the rest still hidden.\n\n“Maybe it only means they know where some stories begin.”\n\nHe considered that.\n\nThen he held out his hand.\n\n“Dance?”\n\n“Did you learn that from Mereana?”\n\n“No. I'm asking you to dance with me. Our own steps. No pretending it's somebody else's tradition.”\n\nI took his hand.\n\nHe tried an exaggerated turn that would have looked heroic if he hadn't nearly stepped into a flower bed. I laughed, pulled him back, and found a gentler rhythm on the lanternlit stones.\n\nThere was nothing ceremonial about it. It was clumsy, private in the middle of a public square, and entirely ours.\n\nMaui watched my face as though waiting for me to decide whether I wanted to keep going.\n\nI did.\n\nThe voices from the gathering rose warmly behind us. The people who had shared their art remained its owners. I carried away an understanding, not a souvenir.\n\nAnd in the soft circle of lanternlight, I chose another dance.\n\nOn the way back from the gathering, I stopped beside the little bridge that led to the bakery. One of the paper lanterns had come loose and was turning in the breeze, tapping its wooden frame against a post.\n\nMaui caught it before it fell.\n\n\"Another heroic rescue,\" he announced. \"You may applaud.\"\n\n\"Careful. You'll encourage yourself.\"\n\n\"Too late.\"\n\nHe hooked the lantern back into place. His hands were broad enough to make the little brass catch look ridiculous. I watched him test it twice before letting go.\n\n\"What?\" he asked.\n\n\"Nothing. I just like that you checked.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"I've been known to take things seriously.\"\n\nBefore I began, I watched Mereana speak with the younger performers while they packed their equipment. One of them asked whether the visitors could repeat a verse outside the planned gathering.\n\n\"We can discuss it with everyone who performed,\" Mereana answered. \"A song offered tonight isn't an invitation for somebody else to decide where we sing tomorrow.\"\n\nMaui went very still beside me.\n\n\"That's fair,\" he said.\n\n\"I thought you'd want an encore.\"\n\n\"I did. But I didn't ask.\"\n\nMereana met his eyes and gave him a small smile. \"You can enjoy what you heard without owning the next performance.\"\n\nI was grateful she'd said it. The evening had been beautiful, and it would have been easy to turn her community's art into scenery for our own relationship.\n\nLater, after the gathering, Maui found me sitting on the low wall by the lanterns. He sat down with enough space between us that it was unmistakably a choice.\n\n\"There's something I don't know how to ask,\" he admitted.\n\n\"Try badly first.\"\n\nHe snorted. \"A very generous invitation.\"\n\n\"I have experience with bad first drafts.\"\n\nHe looked at the lantern casting light across the stones. \"If I danced with you, would you think I was asking for more than a dance?\"\n\n\"I'd ask what you meant.\"\n\n\"Which would ruin my mysterious charm.\"\n\n\"Your mysterious charm has never survived five minutes.\"\n\nHe laughed, then sobered. \"I can take an answer that isn't the one I hoped for.\"\n\n\"I hope so. I don't want anybody guessing a promise from a hand held or a song heard.\"\n\nHe nodded. When I told him I wanted to speak openly about how I loved, he didn't interrupt. For once the lanterns and the music were enough of a stage, and neither of us needed the audience to stay.\n\n\"I know.\" I took a breath. \"There's something I want to tell you while we're still figuring out what this is.\"\n\nHis grin wavered. \"That sounds suspiciously like a speech.\"\n\n\"It might be. Try not to interrupt the important bits.\"\n\n\"I'll be magnificent at listening.\"\n\n\"I've never really wanted an exclusive relationship. I'm ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory.\"\n\nHe lowered the arm he'd been preparing to flourish with.\n\nI went on before courage could become an excuse to retreat. \"I mean honesty. People knowing what they're agreeing to. No sneaking around, no promising someone they're the only person I'll ever love and then hoping they never ask questions. Love doesn't feel smaller to me because it has room for more than one person.\"\n\nMaui was quiet long enough that the tide reached the rocks below us and slid back again.\n\n\"More than one person,\" he repeated.\n\n\"Potentially. I'm not asking you to meet anyone. I'm not saying there is somebody else right now. I am saying I won't pretend to want exclusivity because I like you.\"\n\nHe looked at the lantern, then at me. \"So the part where I show up and make an idiot of myself doesn't give me the right to decide the rest of your life?\"\n\n\"That part is mostly decorative.\"\n\n\"I suspected.\" His smile returned, smaller this time. \"I don't know all the rules.\"\n\n\"Neither do I. We'd have to make agreements together. You could be jealous. I could be jealous. We'd talk instead of treating feelings like crimes.\"\n\nHe sat on the bridge rail, giving me room beside him. \"I've spent a long time wanting people to pick me. The best. The strongest. The only hero.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I think I'd like to find out who I am when someone chooses me without making a trophy out of it.\" He rubbed the back of his neck. \"I'd want honesty, Jasper. Especially when it makes me nervous. And I might need to ask some truly stupid questions.\"\n\n\"I can survive those.\"\n\n\"Bold claim.\"\n\nI took the seat beside him. Our shoulders did not quite touch. Neither of us moved away.\n\n\"Thank you for telling me before I tried to impress you with another dance,\" he said.\n\n\"You are going to do that anyway.\"\n\n\"Obviously.\"\n\nHe offered his hand, palm open, without taking mine. I set my fingers in it because I wanted to.\n\nWe stayed there until the lantern stopped rattling.","title":"A Song for the Unfinished","word_count":1369},"maui-07":{"number":7,"content":"Maui had learned to ask before making plans for me.\n\nHe was worse at asking about himself.\n\nFor three days he had been rebuilding a small landing at Tideglass Cove without admitting it was meant for our canoe. He'd set the planks square, straightened the posts, and then pretended he had happened to wander by whenever I visited.\n\nOn the fourth morning I caught him carving a little pattern along the railing.\n\n“That's a lot of work for a dock nobody asked for.”\n\n“I'm excellent at unrequested excellence.”\n\n“Are you making it for me?”\n\nHe looked at the wood.\n\n“I thought you might like somewhere to come back to.”\n\nMy chest tightened. Not from fear this time.\n\n“I do.”\n\nHe set the knife aside. His tattoos had gone still.\n\n“Can I ask you something?” I said.\n\nHe made a show of squaring his shoulders. “The answer is probably yes.”\n\n“Do you ever get tired of being the hero?”\n\nThe smile disappeared.\n\n“I don't know what I am if I'm not useful.”\n\nI knew how much courage the words cost him. I sat on the edge of the dock, leaving room for him to decide whether to sit too.\n\n“I'm starting to remember things,” I said. “Not names. Expectations. People needing answers before I had time to think. I think I learned to make myself useful because I was afraid nobody would keep me otherwise.”\n\nMaui lowered himself beside me.\n\n“That's a terrible bargain,” he said.\n\n“It's the one I knew.”\n\nHe watched the waves for a long moment.\n\n“Sometimes I think the tattoos are all anyone really wants.”\n\nI reached toward his hand and stopped. “May I?”\n\nHe turned his palm upward.\n\nHis hand was warm and enormous around mine. He didn't look like a legend then. He looked like someone who had carried a heavy thing so long he no longer knew where to put it down.\n\n“You can be here without fixing anything,” I told him.\n\n“So can you.”\n\nI smiled. “We'll probably both need reminding.”\n\nThe landing wasn't quite finished. That was the part Maui kept hiding.\n\nPip came by that afternoon with a ruler and a small bundle of fasteners. They leaned against the new railing, tested its height with a careful hand, and frowned.\n\n\"That won't work for someone sitting down,\" they said.\n\nMaui looked at the wood. \"It's stronger than the old one.\"\n\n\"That's good. But somebody still needs to reach it.\"\n\nI saw the moment his pride took the blow. For three days he had been building something generous, and now a person who hadn't watched him lift a single plank was telling him it had a flaw.\n\n\"Could we add a lower rail?\" I asked.\n\nPip nodded. \"And leave the turning space at the end clear. Don't put that carving where people need to pass.\"\n\nMaui looked at the little pattern he'd started.\n\n\"Even the fish?\"\n\n\"Especially the fish,\" Pip said, unimpressed.\n\nHe stared at the knife. Then he gathered the measuring tools and asked Pip where the second support should go.\n\nI stayed to hold the board while they worked. It was too long for one pair of hands and too awkward for a grand gesture; we had to stop, measure again, and adjust the post by less than an inch.\n\nAt one point Maui said, \"If I move the whole dock, it'll line up.\"\n\nPip didn't even look up. \"If you move the whole dock, Fen will make you move it back.\"\n\nHe laughed, but he left the dock where it was.\n\nWhen the second rail was fixed, Pip tried it from the low stool we'd brought down. They could reach the support without leaning over the water.\n\n\"Better,\" they said.\n\nMaui exhaled as though he'd been waiting for a verdict.\n\nPip packed their things. \"I'll bring Edda tomorrow to test the approach if she wants to. Until then, please don't call it open.\"\n\n\"Understood,\" Maui said.\n\nAfter they left, he rested his hands on the rail.\n\n\"I thought I'd made you somewhere to come back to.\"\n\n\"You have,\" I said. \"You also made a place other people might want to use.\"\n\nHe was quiet.\n\n\"I keep wanting to give you something enormous,\" he admitted. \"A ship, a mountain, the horizon.\"\n\nI rested an elbow on the post, careful not to lean on the part we'd just repaired.\n\n\"Could you give me something smaller?\"\n\n\"Anything.\"\n\n\"Next time, ask me what I want before you start building.\"\n\nHe looked wounded for half a second. Then the expression turned rueful.\n\n\"That does sound harder.\"\n\n\"It does.\"\n\nHe brushed sawdust from his arm.\n\n\"I'll try.\"\n\nI believed him because he'd already spent an afternoon trying something that made his work better without making him the hero of it.\n\nThe landing was finished before the dance, but Lio asked us to check the boards once more. It was her skiff that would use the little dock most often, and she knew exactly where a loose plank would catch a rope.\n\nMaui tried to declare it perfect. Lio looked at him until he sighed and fetched the level.\n\n\"It's meant to be for us,\" he whispered while we worked.\n\n\"I know. And she's meant to be able to use it when we aren't here.\"\n\nHe examined the narrow step. It was an inconvenient height for a heavy crate, so we added a safer place to set a load before carrying it aboard. Lio tested it, thanked us, and went back to her own repairs without asking whose romance had inspired the planks.\n\nOnly then did Maui take my hand.\n\n\"I had planned a much grander reveal,\" he said.\n\n\"I gathered.\"\n\n\"There were going to be flowers.\"\n\n\"Flowers are lovely.\"\n\n\"And a speech.\"\n\n\"Ah. There we are.\"\n\nHe laughed, but there was an ache behind it. I pressed my thumb against his palm and waited.\n\n\"I thought if I did something extraordinary,\" he said, \"you wouldn't have to wonder whether I cared.\"\n\n\"I don't doubt that you care. I want us to be able to ask each other for ordinary things too.\"\n\n\"Such as?\"\n\n\"Tell me when you're tired. Tell me if you want a day together. Don't build a dock instead of asking me whether I need one.\"\n\nHe gazed at the water for a long moment. \"I wanted to make somewhere you could return.\"\n\n\"Then ask whether I want to come.\"\n\nHe looked at me, finally smiling. \"Would you?\"\n\n\"Yes. Not every day. I have other parts of my life and other people I may love. But yes.\"\n\nHe nodded, taking the answer without trying to turn it into forever. At Lanternwell that evening, his invitation to dance sounded like a question instead of a claim. I stepped into his arms because I wanted to.\n\nThat evening we returned to the place beside the Lanternwell where we had danced after Mereana's performance. There was no audience and no music beyond the wind in the trees.\n\nMaui bowed with theatrical extravagance. “May I have this dance?”\n\n“You've been practicing.”\n\n“I am a natural.”\n\n“You nearly stepped into a flower bed last time.”\n\n“A strategic maneuver.”\n\nI took his hand. Our steps were better, or at least less dangerous to the plants. He let me lead for a little while; I let him spin me when I wanted to be spun. A strand of hair blew across my face, and Maui stopped in the middle of a step.\n\n\"Is something wrong?\"\n\n\"I can't see.\"\n\n\"Oh. That seems relevant.\"\n\nHe held still while I tucked the hair back. His hand remained open, waiting, and I could feel how badly he wanted the moment to be perfect.\n\n\"You're allowed to be awkward,\" I told him.\n\n\"I am a legendary dancer.\"\n\n\"Your foot is on my shoe.\"\n\nHe looked down.\n\n\"So it is.\"\n\nWe both laughed. The sound carried across the little square, but no one came to watch. The festival had ended; the neighbors had their own evenings to return to.\n\nI moved my foot and asked whether he wanted me to lead the next turn.\n\nHe nodded.\n\nWe tried again. It wasn't graceful, but it was ours.\n\nWe stopped whenever one of us needed to adjust.\n\nWhen we came close enough that our foreheads nearly touched, he looked suddenly uncertain.\n\n“I want to kiss you,” he said. “But I don't want it to be another thing you feel you owe someone.”\n\n“You don't have to earn it,” I told him. “And neither do I.”\n\n“Is that a yes?”\n\nI answered him with one.\n\nThe kiss was gentle and imperfect. Maui laughed softly against my forehead afterward, surprised by his own happiness.\n\n“I could get used to this,” he said.\n\n“To dancing?”\n\n“To asking and getting an answer I can trust.”\n\nWe stayed beneath the lanterns until they dimmed. There were no grand vows and no declarations carved into stone.\n\nOnly two people who had learned that an invitation was more meaningful than a demand.","title":"A Dance That Asked","word_count":1497},"maui-08":{"number":8,"content":"The Threadway beneath Lanternwell opened for my key on the first cold morning of the season.\n\nNeri found me standing before it, watching a pale rectangle of light reveal the shape of an ordinary room. Inside was a desk, a chair, a stack of letters, and the faded pattern of a curtain I suddenly recognized.\n\nMy hands went numb.\n\n“That's where I came from,” I said.\n\nNeri nodded. “It is one place you've been.”\n\nNot the place I belonged. Not the place I had to return to. One place.\n\nI asked for time alone.\n\nBeyond the doorway, memory returned with a kind of terrible precision. I saw myself keeping lists for other people, making promises too quickly, laughing when I wanted to say no because laughing was easier than disappointing anyone. I remembered warmth too: a favorite book, rain on the windows, the thrill of building something that worked. My old life had not been entirely misery. That made the choice harder.\n\nThere were people I had loved, even if I couldn't yet recall their faces. There were obligations that belonged to me and others I had accepted because nobody asked whether I wanted them.\n\nThe door did not tell me which was which.\n\nI sat on the floor and cried. Not because the room was terrible, but because it was familiar enough to hurt.\n\nThe room contained evidence of a life I had made, but not a verdict on who I was.\n\nA small pile of mended clothes sat in the drawer beneath the desk. The needlework was uneven. I touched a seam with my thumb and remembered sitting at that desk one rainy evening, sewing too quickly because I had promised the shirt back by morning. I remembered the relief when nobody asked why I'd made the promise in the first place.\n\nBeside the letters was a list of repairs. Every task had a date and a box. Some had been crossed out; others had been copied again on new pages. At the bottom, in handwriting that was unmistakably mine, I had written: *Do it properly or don't do it at all.*\n\nI sank into the chair.\n\nThe sentence sounded like the voice that followed me when I fixed the bridge lanterns. I did not know whether it belonged to someone who had hurt me or to a rule I'd made to survive an impossible week. The difference mattered. I couldn't tell from a page.\n\nI opened one envelope. It contained a thank-you note for helping someone move furniture, written by a person whose name I didn't yet recognize. The note was kind. It did not say I owed them anything.\n\nThat hurt differently.\n\nI had been expecting a past built entirely from bad choices. Instead I found evidence that people had sometimes loved me without demanding labor in return. I had also found the old habit of making each kindness into another task.\n\nI put the letter back.\n\nThrough the curtain I could see a narrow strip of sky, the kind of grey that means rain is nearby. The window latch stuck when I tried to open it. My hand knew precisely how to lift the left corner and turn the catch. For a moment the ease of it made me dizzy.\n\nA memory did not have to be a command.\n\nI could remember the latch without moving back into this room. I could keep the thank-you letter without promising to be available to its author forever. I could return another day, or not.\n\nI went to the threshold and found Neri waiting on the far side of the hall, looking at a book to give me privacy.\n\n\"Can I leave it as it is?\" I asked.\n\n\"The room?\"\n\n\"Everything. I don't want someone sorting it while I'm gone.\"\n\nNeri shut the book. \"We can mark the room as private and preserve it. If you later want help cataloging your own things, you can ask.\"\n\n\"Would I have to explain what I'm keeping?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe word was wonderfully uncomplicated.\n\nI took one small notebook from the desk, not the whole pile. Then I closed the drawer and returned the key to my pocket.\n\n\"I don't want to decide my whole life today,\" I said.\n\nNeri nodded. \"Then don't.\"\n\nIt was almost funny, how much relief fit inside two ordinary words.\n\nAt the doorway I stopped long enough to examine the hinges. The metal was ordinary, worn by years of use. Nothing about it announced that entering would bind me to the place again.\n\n\"Will it stay here if I leave?\" I asked Neri.\n\n\"The door may close between crossings. The room does not vanish because you choose not to enter it.\"\n\nI thought of the letters on the desk. I hadn't read them all. Some were addressed to me, others to people whose names still meant nothing. One envelope had been opened and resealed so many times that the corner had gone soft.\n\n\"I don't want you to sort those for me,\" I said.\n\nNeri nodded. \"Then I won't.\"\n\nWe wrote down what I was taking: one notebook, the little brass key, and the memory of a room that had once held both good days and bad ones. The letters could stay in place until I decided whether I wanted to read them, contact their writers, or ask for help understanding them.\n\nI stood in the corridor until the shaking in my hands eased. When I was ready, I walked back through the Threadway.\n\nWhen I returned to the Lanternwell, Maui was waiting at a respectful distance. He had brought food and set it where I could reach it without having to explain myself.\n\n“Did you remember?” he asked.\n\n“Some of it.”\n\n“Are you going back?”\n\n“Maybe, someday. There are things I need to understand. People I may want to find. But I don't want to go back just because I know the way.”\n\nHe didn't try to tell me what to do.\n\n“I was afraid you'd think I'd become somebody else,” I admitted.\n\nHis laugh was gentle. “Jasper, I'm a shapeshifter. I would be a spectacular hypocrite.”\n\n“I'm serious.”\n\n“So am I.” He crouched beside me. “I liked you when you couldn't tell me your name. I like you now that you can tell me when I'm being an idiot. I'm not measuring you against a stranger in a room I've never seen.”\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n“What if I find out I wasn't very brave?”\n\n“Then we start with the brave thing you did today.”\n\n“What was that?”\n\n“You came back because you wanted to, not because I asked.”\n\nThe door behind us closed quietly. My key did not vanish. The choice would remain available.\n\nThat night I opened the notebook I'd begun in the bakery room. On one page I wrote the things I wanted to remember: the taste of bread, the sound of rain, the satisfaction of repairing a lantern, the way the sea changed color at dusk.\n\nOn the next page I wrote the things I wanted to learn: how to sail better, how to say no without an apology, how to return to an old place without becoming an old fear.\n\nMaui looked over the list and pointed at the final blank line.\n\n“Forgot one.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“How to win an argument with a demigod.”\n\nI picked up my pencil.\n\n“I thought I'd already mastered that.”\n\n“Outrageous.”\n\nI wrote it down anyway.","title":"What the Door Kept","word_count":1240},"maui-11":{"number":11,"content":"The morning after the festival, I found the welcome sign floating face down in the cove.\n\nAt first, I thought someone had played a joke.\n\nMaui had been very proud of that sign. It was carved from a piece of driftwood with a curve like a smile, painted in a blue that matched the thread holding my brass key. WELCOME TO TIDEGLASS COVE, it said. SAFE LANDING AT ALL TIDES, it said beneath.\n\nThe second line had been his idea.\n\nI waded in far enough to reach the sign. The water was cold. A small crab took offense to my ankle. I took offense to the crab.\n\nBy the time I dragged the sign onto the sand, Maui had appeared with a spear in one hand and the expression of a man who had expected to fight something large.\n\n\"Who's attacking?\"\n\n\"The ocean.\"\n\nHe looked at the sign.\n\n\"The ocean has terrible manners.\"\n\n\"The ocean also appears to have won.\"\n\nMaui planted the spear in the sand. His tattoos shifted as he crouched beside the wood.\n\n\"Probably a wave.\"\n\n\"Probably several.\" I pointed toward the dock.\n\nThe outermost post leaned at an angle that had not been there yesterday. Beyond it, the new landing boards sat crooked above the tide. One rope had worn almost through where it passed around the mooring ring.\n\nMaui's smile disappeared.\n\n\"Nobody's hurt,\" he said.\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\nHe stood. \"I'll fix it.\"\n\nI caught the edge of his wrist before he could take three long heroic strides into the water.\n\n\"That's not the same as checking whether it's safe.\"\n\n\"I built it.\"\n\n\"I know. That's why I'm asking you to look.\"\n\nHe stared down at my hand. I let go. His pride had begun to rise, hot and quick, but so had his worry.\n\nHe looked at the dock again.\n\n\"All right,\" he said, more quietly. \"We look.\"\n\nWe fetched Fen Arlow, the canal steward, who arrived carrying a measuring stick and an expression that suggested he had already heard about our sign.\n\n\"Safe at all tides,\" he read.\n\n\"I would like to submit an amendment,\" I said.\n\nFen stepped onto the first plank, pressed it with the heel of his boot, and stepped back.\n\n\"Good instinct. The outer post is seated in sand, not stone. The channel shifts after the first hard autumn tide.\"\n\nMaui folded his arms. \"It held through the festival.\"\n\n\"Your festival happened at low tide.\"\n\n\"It was a very successful festival.\"\n\n\"Congratulations. The sea doesn't attend council meetings.\"\n\nI nearly smiled. Maui did not.\n\nFen showed us the dark line water had left against the rocks. It was well above the welcome sign's promised safe level.\n\n\"We need a new post,\" Fen said. \"Maybe two. And until then, that landing is closed.\"\n\nMaui picked up the sign. \"I can replace the whole thing before lunch.\"\n\n\"With what timber?\" Fen asked.\n\n\"The trees.\"\n\n\"Which trees?\"\n\n\"The—\" Maui gestured toward the hillside. \"Trees.\"\n\n\"Somebody owns those. Somebody else planted the young ones to stop the bank from sliding. You cannot solve one problem by creating another.\"\n\nMaui's face did something complicated.\n\nI recognized the moment. He was being told that strength was not enough, and he was struggling not to hear it as proof that he was not enough.\n\n\"I'll find the wood,\" he said.\n\nFen nodded. \"Good. Get permission. Bring it to the yard. We'll test it.\"\n\nThat afternoon the three of us walked the shoreline with Tamsin Vale, the lanternwright, who had once helped maintain the cove steps. Her tool bag clinked when she moved.\n\n\"How did your sign end up in the water?\" she asked.\n\n\"Poor leadership,\" I said.\n\nMaui shot me a look.\n\n\"Not just yours,\" I added. \"Mine too. I approved the wording.\"\n\nTamsin touched the scraped paint with a thumbnail. \"Then you can both approve the correction.\"\n\nWe spent an hour marking tide heights on the stone steps. Another hour counting what the cove actually had: four sound ropes, one damaged mooring ring, a stack of timber too narrow for the outer rail, two usable lantern brackets and a remarkably stubborn crab that refused to vacate the supply basket.\n\nBy sunset, my notebook held three pages of disappointing facts.\n\nMaui stood over my shoulder.\n\n\"I thought this place would be easier.\"\n\n\"Because it's magical?\"\n\n\"Because it wanted us here.\"\n\nI closed the notebook. \"A place can welcome us and still need maintenance.\"\n\nHe sat beside me on the step. The wind lifted his hair from his shoulders. Salt had dried along one edge of his tattooed arm.\n\n\"I wanted to build you somewhere you could come back to,\" he said.\n\n\"You did.\"\n\n\"It broke.\"\n\n\"The dock broke. The choice didn't.\"\n\nHe watched the dark water.\n\nI leaned my shoulder against his. For once, he did not make a speech about being impressive. He leaned back.\n\nFen had watched us scrape the false promise from the sign. They came down from the upper path with a small slate of tide times and asked where we'd put the new information.\n\n\"On a separate board?\" I suggested.\n\n\"Yes. Somewhere people can check it before they launch.\"\n\nMaui looked at the little crab he'd painted. \"But the sign is beautiful.\"\n\n\"It can be beautiful,\" Fen said. \"It can't be a substitute for a changing water chart.\"\n\nWe set a narrow notice beneath the shelter with room for the day's safe landing window, the name of whoever checked it, and a clear route to the upper path when the cove was closed. Lio tried reading it while carrying a crate. She made us move it closer to the place where a person actually needed to decide.\n\nMaui stood back, frowning. \"People are going to think I didn't know what I was doing.\"\n\n\"You didn't,\" Lio said cheerfully.\n\nHe stared at her. She stared back.\n\nThen he laughed.\n\nI wrote down that the weather could change after the morning check and that anyone at the landing should follow the posted closure, even if the water looked calm. We hadn't invented a way to make the sea predictable. We'd made one small choice easier for the people who used it.\n\nMaui touched the blue thread at my wrist with one finger. \"Will you go through the doors again?\"\n\n\"Yes. When I want to.\"\n\n\"I might feel lonely.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to. I'm ethically non-monogamous, and I prefer polyamory. Our choosing each other doesn't mean I won't choose to visit people elsewhere.\"\n\nHe took a breath. \"Can I ask when you'll be back without sounding like a guard?\"\n\n\"You can ask. I can tell you what I know. We don't have to pretend a guess is a promise.\"\n\nHe thought that over, then held out the paintbrush. \"Will you help me make a better crab?\"\n\n\"The crab is excellent.\"\n\n\"I want a second opinion.\"\n\nI leaned against him, looking at the tiny creature beneath the truthful sign. It had survived the tide and our argument. I was beginning to think that was more than enough for a little piece of driftwood.\n\n\"People keep asking you what the doors mean,\" he said. \"Who you'll visit. How often you'll come back.\"\n\n\"People can be curious. It doesn't mean they get to vote.\"\n\n\"I know.\" He hesitated. \"I'm trying to remember what you said. About loving people without locking them up.\"\n\nI touched the little brass key at my throat. \"I meant it.\"\n\n\"So did I. About wanting to try, even when the questions are stupid.\"\n\n\"Do you have one?\"\n\nHe took a breath.\n\n\"If somebody else builds you something that doesn't break, would you stop wanting to come here?\"\n\nIt hurt, because I could hear how carefully he had made the question small.\n\n\"Maui.\" I turned toward him. \"I want to come here because this is home. Not because the dock is perfect, and not because somebody won me at a festival.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"And if I love someone else someday, I'm still responsible for what I promise you. I don't get to leave you guessing. Neither do you.\"\n\nHe let that settle.\n\n\"All right.\"\n\n\"All right?\"\n\n\"I think I can live with a promise that involves actual work.\"\n\n\"Revolutionary.\"\n\nHe grinned, finally.\n\nThen he leaned down to kiss me, stopped close enough for his breath to warm my cheek, and waited.\n\n\"Yes,\" I whispered.\n\nHis lips were soft despite every boast he'd ever made about them. He smelled like salt and the wood shavings caught in his clothes. The first kiss of the evening became a second when I tugged lightly at his hand.\n\nSomewhere above us, the first lantern came on.\n\nWe stayed until it was too dark to count posts.\n\nThe next morning, before the bakery opened, we took the welcome sign to the workshop. Tamsin sanded away the damaged line while I painted new letters in careful white strokes.\n\nWELCOME TO TIDEGLASS COVE.\n\nCHECK THE TIDE CHART BEFORE LANDING.\n\nMaui regarded the result.\n\n\"Less dramatic.\"\n\n\"More accurate.\"\n\n\"I could add a sea monster.\"\n\n\"On the safety sign?\"\n\n\"Only a small one.\"\n\nI handed him the brush.\n\nHe painted a tiny crab at the bottom instead.\n\nI liked it better that way.\n\nFor the first time, the sign told the truth. And when we carried it back down to the shore, nobody had to pretend that made the water harmless.","title":"The Tide Takes Inventory","word_count":1558}}};for(const s of lib.series){const e=revisions[s.series_slug]||{};for(const c of s.chapters){const r=e[c.id];if(!r||c.chapter_number!==r.number)continue;c.content=r.content;c.title=r.title;c.word_count=r.word_count;}}})();
